fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1126", description: some(value: "GATE — 0.1.0 BASE INTERPRETER FEATURE FREEZE. The dependency anchor for work that would CHANGE the base model rather than build on it. WI-010 (type-unification helpers as anthill rules) depends on this, and through WI-010 so does WI-382 (unification as a framework of per-sort registered algorithms). WI-370 (custom unification at a discrimination-tree node) only CITES WI-010 and carries no dependency edge today — checked, not assumed; whether it belongs behind this gate is one of the calls the review makes. Ships no feature of its own: it ships a SETTLED BASE and a TRIAGED proposal backlog, so that everything after it may assume the base model instead of re-litigating it.\n\nWHY NOW, measured rather than felt: the `typing` sequence is 90 items and everything in it is Delivered except WI-382 (blocked on WI-010, i.e. blocked on this), WI-403 (blocked on WI-002) and WI-590 (in flight). The base type system is written. What is NOT settled is which of the 60 numbered proposals it was written AGAINST — and that silence is what WI-010 would otherwise inherit, since rebuilding the typer as anthill rules against an untriaged backlog rewrites a model nobody has agreed on.\n\nTARGET STATE, four parts.\n\n(1) THE BASE TYPE SYSTEM IS WRITTEN DOWN AS THE MODEL. docs/kernel-language.md describes the type system the 0.1.0 interpreter actually implements, as one normative model rather than an accumulation of per-ticket paragraphs. The test is negative and checkable: no section of the BASE carries a `not enforced today` / `WI-NNN decides which side moves` hole (WI-884's connective rule is the live example, §5.3). Holes outside the base are fine and must be LISTED, not silently left.\n\n(2) EVERY PROPOSAL CARRIES A VERDICT. The population is docs/proposals: 60 main-sequence numbers (66 files with the .1 sub-numbers), plus library/ (8) and future/ (4). Each gets exactly one verdict — IMPLEMENTED (in the base; the spec absorbed it, and per repo rule the proposal KEEPS its own text), COMMITTED-NOT-YET-BUILT (stays numbered, still on the roadmap past 0.1.0), MOVED TO future/ (deferred, unnumbered), or REJECTED (with the reason, which is the part that stops it being re-proposed). A proposal with no verdict is exactly the defect this gate removes: today silence reads as `committed` for all 60, and no reader can tell an implemented proposal from an aspirational one without reading the implementation.\n\n(3) FUTURE FUNCTIONALITY IS IN future/, REJECTED WORK IS IN rejected/ AND SAYS WHY. Both directories exist and both document their conventions; future/README.md had only the PROMOTION direction (a sketch becomes committed, takes the next number, moves up) and now carries DEMOTION beside it.\n\nDECIDED, and already written down, so the review does not have to stop for it: THE NUMBER TRAVELS WITH THE FILE. A numbered proposal that is deferred moves to docs/proposals/future/NNN-....md; one that is declined moves to docs/proposals/rejected/NNN-....md; either way the number is RETIRED, never reused, and the DIRECTORY carries the verdict. The constraint that settles it is back-references: kernel-language.md, other proposals, and ~1000 work-item descriptions all cite proposals BY NUMBER (`058 §4.9`, `proposal 051 Phase 2`), so a number that changed meaning, or a file that moved out from under its number, would silently falsify text nobody re-reads. Keeping the number in the filename makes every existing citation still resolve, to a file whose header now says it was declined or deferred and why. The rules live in docs/proposals/rejected/README.md (created with this ticket, including what a rejection MUST say -- notably `what would have to change for this to be reconsidered`, which is what separates a rejection that invites the proposal back from one that does not) and in future/README.md beside the promotion rule.\n\n(4) THE BASE MODEL IS FIXED. After this gate a change to the base type model is a PROPOSAL against 0.1.x, not an edit — the same discipline the spec already applies to itself. State where the line is: which constructs are base (sorts, rules, operations, the four kernel constructs, unification, the eq family, effect rows) and which are extensions that may still move.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. Not a code freeze: bug fixes, diagnostics, and the review-driven repairs this repo does inline all continue. Not a release — publishing to crates.io is WI-165, a different axis, and it may proceed before or after. Not a promise that 0.1.0 is complete; only that its base model has stopped moving.\n\nACCEPTANCE.\n  * Every file under docs/proposals (including library/ and future/) carries a verdict DISCOVERABLE WITHOUT READING THE FILE — an index row, a header, or the directory it sits in. The count of proposals with no verdict is ZERO, and what says so is a script or a test, not a reading: an inventory that only names what a human noticed is the failure mode this repo has hit before.\n  * Every moved or rejected proposal follows the number rule of (3), and every file in rejected/ carries the header that README requires -- including the `what would have to change` half, without which a rejection is just a deletion with extra steps.\n  * kernel-language.md's base type-system sections have no unresolved-decision holes; any hole outside the base is listed in one place.\n  * cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh; scaland sbt test green.\n\nCHILDREN. File them AGAINST this ticket as the review proceeds rather than up front — the review's own output is what says how many there are and which proposals need real work rather than a verdict line. Per repo rule, a verdict small enough to write inline is written inline, not spun into a ticket."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)

